<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Owari Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1607 CE – 1871 CE · capital Nagoya</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The senior of the three Tokugawa branch houses, ruling from Nagoya castle and entitled to supply an heir if the shogun's line died out.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari</a> to his ninth son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinao">Tokugawa Yoshinao</a> in 1607. In 1610 Ieyasu ordered lords across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a> castle, and the whole town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kiyosu">Kiyosu</a> was moved there, creating the city of Nagoya.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With about 619,500 koku the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari</a> Tokugawa were the highest-ranking of the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gosanke">Gosanke</a> houses, yet no Owari lord ever became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-muneharu">Tokugawa Muneharu</a> defied Shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Yoshimune</a>'s austerity by encouraging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, festivals and commerce, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagoya">Nagoya</a> thrive, until he was forced to retire in 1739. The domain was rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> from the Kiso valley and ceramics from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seto-aichi">Seto</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshikatsu">Tokugawa Yoshikatsu</a> sided with the new government and purged pro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> retainers. After abolition in 1871 the domain became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aichi-prefecture">Aichi Prefecture</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/owari-domain.jpg" alt="Owari Domain" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Barfodt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Tokugawa_Lords_of_Nagoya_in_Owari_Province_(A).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1607 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nagoya</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tokugawa Yoshinao, Tokugawa Muneharu, Tokugawa Yoshikatsu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nagoya Domain, Owari han</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1739&amp;focus=owari-domain" aria-label="Owari Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8147 905 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Owari Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8467 1161L8530 1145M8467 1161L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8467 1161h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8467 1161h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Nagoya. <a href="/atlas?year=1739&amp;focus=owari-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
